= Language — Technical =

== Object of linguistic theory ==
"Linguistic language" is not ontologically neutral.

Prominent research traditions emphasize different objects:

* externalized public patterns and corpora;
* idiolectal or speaker-internal competence;
* abstract grammatical structures;
* socially sustained conventions;
* usage events and learned constructions.

These perspectives can support overlapping empirical predictions while disagreeing about explanatory primitives.

== Levels of representation ==
A schematic linguistic architecture may distinguish:

<pre>
signal
 ↓
phonetic / gestural representation
 ↓
phonological structure
 ↓
morphology
 ↓
syntax
 ↓
semantics
 ↕
pragmatics / discourse / context
</pre>

The interfaces are not cleanly modular in every theory. Prosody, information structure, lexical choice, morphology, syntax, semantics, and pragmatics can interact.

== Productivity and systematicity ==
Productivity requires mechanisms capable of generalizing beyond memorized utterances. Formal grammars model this with generative or constraint-based systems; usage-based and constructional approaches emphasize learned form–meaning pairings and distributional generalization.

No single formal architecture follows merely from the fact of productivity.

== Competence, performance, and usage ==
Some theories distinguish an idealized underlying linguistic competence from performance limitations. Other approaches treat actual usage distributions, processing constraints, interaction, and learning as constitutive evidence about grammar.

Corpus frequencies therefore need neither be dismissed as "mere performance" nor treated as direct grammatical rules without theory.

== Typology and modality ==
Cross-linguistic and signed-language evidence constrains claims about universals. A proposed universal that depends on properties specific to speech or alphabetic writing is not automatically a language universal.

== Natural versus formal language ==
Formal languages typically have explicitly specified alphabets, formation rules and interpretation rules.

Natural languages are historically evolved, socially embedded, variable, context-sensitive systems whose grammars are inferred from evidence rather than legislated by a complete formal specification.

